  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>We know that the gravitational field strength varies according to the variation of the distance of an object&nbsp;from the centre of the earth.</p><p>So, gravitational field strength inside the earth varies as,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac></math>&nbsp;if&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#8804;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8804;</mo><mi>R</mi></math>.</p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mi>r</mi></math>.</p><p>At the surface, it is maximum,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>G</mi><mi>M</mi></mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>.</p><p>Outside the earth, it varies as,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math>&nbsp;for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8805;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>)</mo></math>.</p><p>So, this is the possible graph,</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/9e0bfffc-5b81-493f-a78c-6d20681f42bd-image.png\" style=\"width: 180px; height: 156px;\" /></p>",
    "image": null
  },
